<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of La Marche</h1><p class="st-meta">958 CE – 1527 CE (approx.) · capital Guéret</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A border county between Poitou and the Limousin, held by the Lusignans at the height of their power and later by Armagnac and Bourbon princes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> took its name from its position as a frontier zone, or march, of the duchy of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a>. Around 958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boso-the-old">Boso the Old</a>, a lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charroux">Charroux</a>, held comital authority there, and his descendants ruled both La Marche and the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-perigord">Périgord</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A poor upland county of pasture and woodland, La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> mattered for its strategic place between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">Plantagenet</a> and Capetian spheres. From 1199 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">Lusignans</a> held it; Hugh X married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-of-angouleme">Isabella of Angoulême</a>, widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>, and joined a failed revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX</a> in 1242. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gueret">Guéret</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charroux">Charroux</a> were its main centres.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Lusignan line ended in 1308, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV</a> took the county. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-armagnac">Armagnacs</a> and back to the Bourbons as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> confiscated the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-duke-of-bourbon">Constable Charles of Bourbon</a> in 1523. It was united with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> in 1527.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-la-marche.jpg" alt="County of La Marche" width="320" height="382"><figcaption>Image: Reigen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_1154-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>958 CE – 1527 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guéret</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boso the Old, Hugh X of Lusignan, Jacques d'Armagnac</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>La Marche</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1242&amp;focus=county-of-la-marche" aria-label="County of La Marche on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4715 550 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of La Marche lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5035 806L5035 888M5035 806L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5035 806h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5035 806h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1242&amp;focus=county-of-la-marche">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
